'''''La Lesbiana Impopular''''' ({{w|English language|English}}: "The Unpopular Lesbian") is a 2007 [[Cinema of Balboa|Balboan]] {{w|comedy-drama}} film directed by [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]]. A fictional autobiographical work, the film transposes Riera i Sola's own experience of growing up as a lesbian teenager in [[Sant Bart]] in the 1960s into a 1990s version of the same city, producing deliberate {{w|anachronism|anachronisms}} that underscore the disjunction between the protagonist's internal world and her external reality. The film was Riera i Sola's second film after her return to filmmaking following ''[[Les Animes]]'' (2006) and was regarded by critics as a return to the subversive energy of her 1980s underground work.

== Premise ==

The film follows a teenage girl in [[Sant Bart]] in the late 1990s who is mentally situated in the 1960s. She is a lesbian and lives in fear of persecution, expecting hostility, rejection, and punishment for her sexuality — the responses she would have faced in the conservative Sant Bart of the 1960s, where Riera i Sola herself grew up. The Sant Bart of the 1990s, however, is a more accepting and cosmopolitan city: her peers, her family, and her community are largely indifferent to or supportive of her sexuality. The comedy and the pathos of the film derive from the gap between the protagonist's internal fears, shaped by a conservative upbringing that no longer corresponds to the world around her, and the acceptance she actually encounters.

The film's deliberate anachronisms reinforce this disjunction. The [[No Return movement]] protests of the 1950s and 1960s — a coalition opposing the return of [[Sant Cristòfor]] to [[Balboa]] — appear in the film's 1990s setting as "No Return to Black and White TV," a protest movement against the return of obsolete television technology. The trivialisation of a political movement into a consumer complaint mirrors the protagonist's own displacement: the fears and political realities of one decade persist in the consciousness of a person living in another, where those fears no longer correspond to reality.

== Cast ==

* [[Clara Mundó i Riera]] as the protagonist, a teenage girl in Sant Bart in the 1990s who is mentally situated in the 1960s
* [[Berta Casals i Ribera]] as her best friend
* [[Dolors Aymar i Puig]] as her mother
* [[Gerard Trias i Vell]] as a teacher

== Production ==

The film was shot digitally on a budget of approximately $45,000, with support from the Balboan Arts Council. Riera i Sola wrote and directed the film and also served as its editor. The cinematography was by Núria Bosch i Cardona. The film was produced independently and distributed through independent cinemas in [[Sant Cristòfor]].

== Release and reception ==

The film received a limited theatrical release in Sant Cristòfor in 2007 and was screened at the [[Unió d'Artesans del Cinema|UAC]] Awards ceremony. It achieved moderate commercial success, grossing approximately $180,000 — a stronger commercial performance than Riera i Sola's subsequent film ''[[El Desig 2 (film)|El Desig 2]]'' (2010), though still modest by mainstream standards.

Critics regarded the film as a return to form for Riera i Sola, who had been largely absent from filmmaking throughout the 1990s and early 2000s. The film's blend of autobiographical material, absurdist anachronism, and coming-of-age comedy was seen as recapturing the subversive energy of her 1980s underground work, particularly ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' (1987), while operating in a more accessible register. Reviewers in ''[[La Veu de la Badia]]'' and ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'' praised the film's central conceit — the protagonist's fear of a persecution that does not exist — as both comic and quietly devastating.

The film attracted some controversy in the interior provinces, where commentators in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] criticised the film's trivialisation of the [[No Return movement]] through the "No Return to Black and White TV" anachronism. Riera i Sola did not respond publicly to the criticism.

